      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Typically, long-term, \"original\" residents might have lower average incomes, be renters, and belong to historically marginalized groups. Conversely, the incoming demographic is often characterized by young professionals or affluent individuals with higher educational attainment and disposable income. This socioeconomic contrast is central to gentrification, as the newcomers' purchasing power drives the changes that eventually push out the established community. Understanding this shift from working-class or low-income tenants to higher-earning populations is key to analyzing displacement.",
      "source": "target",
