      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Academically, the cultural shift is often framed using concepts like \"aestheticization\" or \"curation\" of the local character. Planners might use positive language like \"revitalization\" or \"enhanced vibrancy\" to describe the influx of new businesses and amenities. Conversely, long-term residents may describe the changes negatively, using terms suggesting displacement, cultural erasure, or loss of \"authenticity.\" This narrative tension between perceived \"improvement\" and experienced loss is a critical element in urban sociology.",
